//~ ErrorDupName:1 "conflicting definition"
// What the module tree refuses, and where each refusal is reported.
//
// The whole scenario is the folder, because what is under test is which folder
// draws a module and which does not, and most of the diagnostics are about files
// this one never names.
//
// Parse stage, which is why it is its own scenario: a parse diagnostic stops the
// compile before name resolution, so nothing here resolves.
//
// The folder holds:
//   sub/sub.cone             a submodule, which the import below may not name
//                            and a declaration below may not share a name with
//   bare/bare.cone           a submodule with no 'mod' line, which is refused,
//                            and whose name a declaration below may not share
//   module_submodule_reject/module_submodule_reject.cone
//                            a submodule named like this module
//   org/deep/deep.cone       a designated file too deep to draw a module
//
// The diagnostic with no line of its own is in cases.toml: which folder draws a
// module is a fact about the filesystem, not a position in a source.
//
// WHERE A MODULE NAMED BY ITS FOLDER IS REPORTED. A module is made positioned at
// the first line of its designated file, the file that makes the folder a
// module, and its 'mod' line moves it there once the file is parsed. A name
// bound before that -- this module's own, meeting the subfolder named like it,
// which is drawn before any file of the tree is parsed -- is reported at the
// first line, so the annotation on line 1 above is this module's half of that
// collision, 'mod' line or no. A module whose designated file has no 'mod' line
// is refused, and stays positioned at its first line (bare/bare.cone).

mod module_submodule_reject;

// A subfolder holding its own designated file is already a module of this one,
// bound under the name its folder gives it. Importing it asks for a second
// binding of a name this module has -- and says a file is reached by naming it,
// where the whole of the tree is that the folder is what brings it in
import sub;        //~ ErrorModFile "submodule of this one"

fn designated() i64 {
  1i64;
}

// Nor may a declaration of this module spell a submodule's name. The submodule
// is bound before any of this module's files is parsed, so the declaration is
// the duplicate, and the submodule's half is reported where it declares itself:
// at its 'mod' declaration in sub/sub.cone
fn sub() i64 {       //~ ErrorDupName:1 "already defined"
  2i64;
}

// The same for a submodule with no declaration: its half is reported at the
// first line of bare/bare.cone
fn bare() i64 {      //~ ErrorDupName:1 "already defined"
  3i64;
}
